From 92fefa86c6297d7e21cd8adcf582267177d611f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 14:33:18 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] Keep the chroma resolution and the bits of high bit depth
 video

The FFmpeg backend turned every decoded format that Qt Multimedia has no
pixel format for into one that it has, and chose the nearest by only the
bits per sample: 4:4:4 and 4:2:2 video at more than 8 bits became P016, a
4:2:0 format, and at 8 bits (4:4:4) YUV420P, so the U and V planes lost
resolution for good (one pixel wide coloured lines came out purple), and
hardware decoding was taken for a stream whatever the bits of the frames its
decoder makes.

Add the pixel formats that hold them: Format_YUV444P (8 bits), and
Format_YUV422P16 and Format_YUV444P16 (16 bit samples, the bits of a video of
fewer in the upper ones, as P016 holds them), with the texture description,
a shader that is the one of YUV420P10 without its scale, and the plane
layout of a frame in memory. The FFmpeg backend maps formats of that chroma
resolution to them (swscale does the change of the bit count, the chroma is
not touched), and does not decode in hardware when the frames the hardware
decoder makes would have fewer bits per sample than the stream, where the
software decoder keeps them all.

The CPU conversion to an image (QVideoFrame::toImage without rendering by
the GPU) does not know the new formats.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
